1. In our project, we use Prometheus to collect metrics and Grafana to visualize application and infrastructure health.

2. Prometheus gathers metrics from exporters such as Node Exporter, Blackbox Exporter, and kube-state-metrics, 
which are configured as scrape targets in the prometheus.yml file, either as static targets or via Kubernetes service discovery.

3. These exporters expose system metrics, Kubernetes metrics, and availability metrics,
Prometheus stores this data with the time it was collected as time-series data.

4. I have created Grafana dashboards by configuring Prometheus as a data source, writing PromQL queries,
and also importing existing dashboards using Grafana dashboard IDs.

5. We define Prometheus rules in separate YAML files, including recording rules to pre-compute frequently used metrics for improved query performance.

6. We also define alerting rules to continuously evaluate conditions such as sustained CPU or memory pressure; 
these rule files are referenced from prometheus.yml.

7. When an alert fires, Prometheus sends it to Alertmanager along with labels like pod, namespace, and severity.

8. Alertmanager handles grouping, deduplication, and routing  notifications to slack or pager duty.

9. Alertmanager integrates with Slack via webhooks, Email via SMTP, and PagerDuty using API integration keys.

This setup helps us detect issues early, reduce downtime, and respond to incidents faster across environments.
dashboard queries:

1.Node / Infrastructure Metrics
CPU Usage %
100 - (avg by (instance) (
rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])
) * 100)
___________________________--
2.Memory Usage %
(node_memory_MemTotal_bytes - node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes)
/ node_memory_MemTotal_bytes * 100
____________________________________________
3.Disk Usage %
100 - (
node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint="/"} 
/ node_filesystem_size_bytes{mountpoint="/"} * 100
)
______________________________________________
4.Network Traffic (RX/TX)
rate(node_network_receive_bytes_total[5m])
rate(node_network_transmit_bytes_total[5m])
_______________________________________
5.Kubernetes Metrics
Pod Status
kube_pod_status_phase

6.Pod Restarts
increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[5m])

7.Running Pods
count(kube_pod_status_phase{phase="Running"})

8.Pending Pods
count(kube_pod_status_phase{phase="Pending"})

9.Pod CPU Usage
sum by (pod) (
rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total[5m])
)

10.Pod Memory Usage
sum by (pod) (
container_memory_working_set_bytes
)

11.Node Not Ready
kube_node_status_condition{condition="Ready",status="false"}

🚀 Application Metrics
12.Request Rate
sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))

13.Error Rate
sum(rate(http_requests_total{status=~"5.."}[5m]))

14.Latency (95th percentile)
histogram_quantile(0.95,
sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])) by (le)
)

☁ AWS Metrics (CloudWatch Exporter)
15.EC2 CPU Utilization
aws_ec2_cpuutilization_average

16.ALB Request Count
aws_applicationelb_request_count_sum

17.RDS CPU
aws_rds_cpuutilization_average

18.⚠ Alert Useful Queries
High CPU
100 - avg(rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])) * 100 > 80

19.Pod CrashLoop
increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[5m]) > 3
